WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
Hardly anything runs in our&nbsp;
society today without microchips.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.820
They are the heart of cell&nbsp;
phones, computers, cars. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.780
A chip is made up of transistors: tiny switches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.960
10,000 times smaller than a human hair that&nbsp;
control the flow of electrons through a circuit.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.760
Billions of transistors are packed&nbsp;
into an area smaller than a fingernail.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.920
Microchips are one of humankind’s&nbsp;
most remarkable achievements.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.260
And it wouldn’t be possible to make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.460
world’s most sophisticated chips&nbsp;
without the German company ZEISS.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.640
In a previous video, I talked about how the&nbsp;
Dutch firm ASML is the only company in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.440
world that builds the machines that create&nbsp;
the circuitry for the most advanced chips.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.060
These machines wouldn’t be able to operate&nbsp;
without ZEISS mirrors inside of them.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.280
These are not your average mirrors.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.300
They’re a complex optical&nbsp;
system that miniaturizes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.060
structure of a chip printed on a silicon wafer.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.140
Light is projected through a blueprint&nbsp;
of the pattern that will be printed.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.620
ZEISS optics focus the pattern onto the wafer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.500
which has already been coated&nbsp;
with a light-sensitive chemical.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:17.280
It shrinks the pattern down to the nanometer&nbsp;
level. A nanometer is one billionth of a meter!

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.780
In the end, the wafer is cut&nbsp;
into small pieces of microchips.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.900
While ASML’s less advanced chipmaking&nbsp;
machines use lenses to focus light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.660
lenses won’t work for its most advanced&nbsp;
lithography machines because these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.980
machines use Extreme Ultraviolet&nbsp;
light, which lenses would absorb.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:43.680
EUV is a shorter wavelength of light than that&nbsp;
used by conventional chip-making machines.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.800
EUV light can help pack more transistors&nbsp;
onto a chip, making the chip more powerful.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.380
Since Zeiss cannot use lenses for the&nbsp;
most advanced machines, it uses mirrors.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:01.260
A series of ultra-reflective curved mirrors&nbsp;
guide the light in a vacuum chamber.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:08.220
The mirrors need to be extremely flat because even&nbsp;
the smallest irregularity leads to imaging errors.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.980
We’re not talking about your&nbsp;
typical level of smoothness.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.980
They are HYPER smooth. Polished to&nbsp;
a thickness of less than one atom!

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.300
What does that even mean?

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.640
Well, if you were to enlarge the mirror&nbsp;
to the size of Germany, then the tallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:31.080
mountain would be less than 1 mm high!
ZEISS makes the flattest mirrors on earth.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.180
They are held in a position as precisely&nbsp;
as possible during the exposure process.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.040
They are so stable that if a ZEISS&nbsp;
mirror were to redirect a laser beam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.080
and aim it at the moon, it would be able&nbsp;
to hit a ping pong ball on its surface!

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.780
ASML knows just how crucial&nbsp;
ZEISS is to its entire operation.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:58.200
It’s no wonder that in 2016, ASML bought&nbsp;
a billion-euro stake in a subsidiary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.760
of Zeiss, Carl Zeiss SMT, purchasing&nbsp;
24.9% of the shares of Carl Zeiss SMT.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.660
ZEISS and ASML pretty much have no rivals.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.860
Every one of the most advanced chips on&nbsp;
Earth has passed through their system.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:17.160
They supply Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing&nbsp;
Company that produces over 90% of the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:24.840
most advanced chips. TSMC makes 5 nm chips and&nbsp;
is striving to make even smaller 3 nm ones.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:30.660
The strategic partnership between Zeiss&nbsp;
and ASML means the barrier to entry for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.420
potential competitors is exceedingly high.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.460
Other manufacturers could try to build&nbsp;
their own EUV lithography machines.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.800
However, if China does want to build a&nbsp;
machine that could rival Zeiss and ASML’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.660
analysts believe it would take at&nbsp;
least 10 years for them to do so.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:54.540
Trouble is, China cannot access the most&nbsp;
advanced equipment from Zeiss and ASML right now.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.080
Because the Trump administration pressed ASML hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.120
to block sales of its most advanced&nbsp;
EUV lithography machines to China.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.200
And the Biden administration has&nbsp;
broadened chip restrictions against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.520
Beijing which I discussed in a previous&nbsp;
video that I’ll link in my description.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.400
Since ZEISS was founded by German&nbsp;
entrepreneur Carl Zeiss in the mid-1800s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.200
it has since grown to secure THE&nbsp;
advantage in optics on the world stage.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.960
Yet it’s not sitting comfortably.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.380
ZEISS is already working on next-generation&nbsp;
optics to by increasing the numerical aperture.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:37.560
The numerical aperture is a measure of how much&nbsp;
light the optical system can collect and focus.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.720
The increase in the numerical aperture&nbsp;
will significantly improve the resolution.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.840
Improving the resolution will increase the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.340
of transistors that can be packed onto&nbsp;
the chip, making it even more powerful.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.760
And as our future moves toward autonomous driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:00.300
artificial intelligence, and 5G and beyond&nbsp;
- you can bet there will be demand for ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.200
more powerful processors and&nbsp;
the machines that make them.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.460
Building the world’s most&nbsp;
powerful lenses and mirrors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.940
would not be possible without talented engineers.

